  "checkFixes": {
    "FIT-4": "measured against C's own exterior (enclosure c-exterior): an open base pixel that reaches C's exterior through transparent pixels is C's outline. The skirt's 9 px (x 622, y 773–781: C's side seam beside the arm–torso gap the flared skirt closes at the hand) → 0. A hole inside a garment never touches C's exterior: a 12 × 12 hole (144 px) and too short bottoms still fail (test).",
    "FIT-6v2": "a crossing by its SHAPE: besides v3's square allowance (≤ 6 × 6), a COMPACT piece — shorter side ≤ 6, longer ≤ 1.5 × shorter (two contours meeting at ≥ 34°) — is a reported crossing. The skirt's hem corner (6 × 7, 24 px) → crossing. An elongated strip along the garment (3 × 20) and a too narrow garment still fail (test); the sandals' 2 × 12 px strip still fails. Limit: an edge bite of the same compact shape cannot be told from a crossing by geometry alone — every compact piece over the square is listed (compactCrossingsOverSquare) for manual review.",
    "skinShadow": "darker = mean channel ratio < 0.98 (D-214: red < 0.98). The khaki hem's cast shadow on the ankle (249,156,95 on C's 250,193,124: k 1.00/0.81/0.77) was taken as fabric and showed as orange specks over the boot. Khaki and brown leather stay fabric (test). Known limit: an orange garment edge over skin can read as shadow.",
    "contourEdgeUnmix": "a garment pixel within 1 px of C's visible, opaque skin that is a mix of the nearest contour ink K and C's own pixel (every channel within 12) becomes K at alpha a: composed on C it is the picture (≤ 12), over another item it is the contour. Not under a closed shoe (the foot is hidden there). Fabric is never unmixed (test)."
  },
  "khakiHemOverBoot": {
    "cause": "the cast shadow under the hem (taken as fabric by D-214's red test) and the contour's antialias against skin",
    "skinTonedPxBefore": 33,
    "skinTonedPxAfter": 2,
    "orangeishPxRemaining": 7,
    "remainingWhy": "a mix of the contour with the SHADOWED skin of the picture, not a clean two-colour mix with C — kept as fabric by the rule (nothing is changed without that evidence)"
  },
